{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>And he said.</b> Many lacking faith have gone astray on account of this verse and said that a person is obligated to observe the day of Shabbat and the night that follows it, for Moses said 'for today is a Shabbat to YHWH,' and not the night that passed; he also said 'tomorrow,' and they interpreted 'and it was evening and it was morning' (Genesis 1:5) according to their desire, that the first day was not complete until the morning of the second day. But they did not speak correctly, for Moses did not speak to Israel except according to their custom, as I have mentioned to you. For the custom of the lands of the uncircumcised is not like the custom of the land of Israel in their food, their clothing, their building, and their affairs, for there is no custom to bake or cook in summer and in winter, nor to do labor except by day. Therefore he said 'tomorrow.' And now set your mind to understand the foolishness of the interpreters regarding 'and it was evening and it was morning' which I have mentioned, for Scripture says 'and Elohim called the light Day' (Genesis 1:5), and it is from the time the sun shines until it sets; and to the darkness He called Night (ibid.), from the time the sun sets until it rises. And so night is the opposite of day, just as darkness is the opposite of light. If so, how can [one] call [it], from the time of evening, which is the setting of the sun, until the morning of the day — behold it is night; and behold it is written concerning these: 'they shall regard the night as belonging to the day' (regarding which see the verse). And know that 'day' in the sacred tongue is in two ways: the first, as I have mentioned — all the time the light of the sun stands upon the earth, corresponding to every place; therefore it is written 'night and day' (Esther 4:16) and 'day and night' (Genesis 8:22), 'three days and three nights' (Jonah 2:1), for if the measure of 'day' included night within it, why mention night? And this is impossible with absolute demonstration from the science of speech, that a noun would include two things one of which is the opposite of the other. And the second way: the word 'day' applies to a near or distant time: 'on the day that I struck Egypt, every firstborn' (Numbers 3:13), 'you are crossing today' (Deuteronomy 9:1), 'to another land as this day' (ibid. 29:27), 'and it shall be on that day' (Isaiah 7:21), and many like these. And now let us set aside all these matters and seek the 'day' of the Torah, as I have said, for our years are not fixed by the course of the sun and the moon but by the determination of the court. And we find that it says: 'Seven days you shall eat unleavened bread' (above, 12:15), and it specified that this count is from the fourteenth of the month in the evening (ibid., 12:18). And it is written: 'from evening to evening you shall observe your Shabbat' (Leviticus 23:32). And furthermore, one upon whom a seminal emission occurs, whether at night or by day — for thus it is written 'if a seminal emission occurs to him at night' (Deuteronomy 23:11) — behold he is not purified until the sun comes, which is the end of the first day; and if it were so, that it is until the morning of the second day, it would be fitting for him to wash in the morning, for they say that the day and the night that follows it are called 'a day' — behold, half the day he is impure and half pure; and one upon whom a seminal emission occurs at the beginning of the night — half the day that passed is impure, and also half the coming day, and these are the words of those who stray. And I have already interpreted 'and it was evening and it was morning' in its place." "<b>And he said.</b> Now he explained what to do with the remainder of what was laid out, and he revealed to them its secret: for they would not find it today, for it would not descend."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {0 "Resolved האשר כתוב על אלה לילה ליום ישימו as a reference to Psalms 139:11, which Ibn Ezra cites elliptically; the phrase 'regarding which see the verse' translates the hyper-literal 'see upon it' since the reference is textually compressed."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:35:23.743458464Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}